\label{sims}
\section{Simulations}

\subsection{Simulation 1}

Our first simulation evaluation is based on the German Breast Cancer Study data (cite Schumacher, JCO 1994) where the outcome of interest 
was tumor recurrence.  The study sample size is $n=686$ and we explore subgroups based on 7 baseline factors:  
$Z_1$=Estrogen, $Z_2$=Age, $Z_3$=Progesteron, $Z_4$=Menopaus, $Z_5$=Positive nodes, $Z_6$=Size, and $Z_7$=Grade.  The true outcome generating model is 

\begin{equation}
\log(T)=\mu+\beta_{0}\hbox{Treat}+\beta_1 Z_1 + \beta_2 Z_2 + \beta_3 Z_3 + \beta_4 Z_4 + \beta_5 Z_5 + \gamma \hbox{Treat}Z_{1}Z_{4} + \epsilon.
\label{dgma}
\end{equation}

The interaction between $Z_1$ and $Z_4$ represents the subgroup H= $\{Z_{1}=1 \}\cap \{Z_{4}=1\}$ which is of size $n_{sg}=84$ and the 
parameters $\beta_{0}$ and $\gamma$ determine the treatment effects where $\gamma=0$ generates no subgroup ($H=\emptyset$). 
The complement $H^{c}$= $\{Z_{1}=0 \}\cup \{Z_{4}=0\}$ therefore represent $n=602$ subjects. 

The parameters $\mu$ and $(\beta_{0},\ldots,\beta_{5})$ 
are based on Weibull model fits to the data and $\gamma$ is chosen to generate causal log-hazard ratio subgroup effects of interest (e.g., $2.0$, $2.5$, etc).  Censoring is also generated by a Weibull model (without the interaction) in order 
to have approximately $\Sexpr{100*round(mean(res_null$p.cens),2)}\%$ censoring (The observed censoring rate in the study was $56\%$ [need to check (remove hard coding of this value)]).


Under the null model with $\gamma=0$ ($H=\emptyset$), $H^{c}$ is the ITT population, with hazard ratio $=\Sexpr{round(dgm.null$hr.Hc.true,2)}$. We note as the subgroup effect $\gamma > 0$ 
varies the overall (ITT) population effect will also vary, except that the subgroup effect for $H^{c}$ will remain constant.  That is, as the 
degree of ``lack of benefit'' for the $H$ subgroup increases, the ``dilution'' of the ITT effect increases, whereas the ``degree of benefit'' for the $H^{c}$ population remains constant.
For example, under the parameters for model (\ref{dgma}), with $\gamma > 0$ chosen to yield subgroup hazard ratios of $\Sexpr{round(dgm.alt1$hr.H.true,2)}$ and 
$\Sexpr{round(dgm.alt2$hr.H.true,2)}$, the ITT effects are $\Sexpr{round(dgm.alt1$hr.causal,2)}$ and $\Sexpr{round(dgm.alt2$hr.causal,2)}$, respectively; Whereas, the 
the hazard ratio for $H^{c}$ is $\Sexpr{round(dgm.alt2$hr.Hc.true,2)}$ for any $\gamma >0$ .

%$\Sexpr{dgm.alt1$hr.Hc.true}$
%$\Sexpr{dgm.alt2$hr.Hc.true}$
%$\Sexpr{dgm.null$hr.causal}$


We evaluate the operating characteristics for identifying and characterizing (estimation) $H$ and $H^{c}$ when the hazard ratio for $H$ takes values of $1, 1.5, 2, 2.5$, and $3.0$ and the overall sample 
size is $500$, $700$, or $1,000$.

In addition to the proposed forest search approach we evaluate the virtual twins (\citealp{FTR_2011}) and generalized random forests (\citealp{ATW_2019,AW_2021}) approaches.  For the virtual twins approach, to account for censoring 
we employ the ''censoring unbiased transformation'' of \citealp{FG_2018}.

\begin{enumerate}
\item{For each of the approaches we restrict to subgroups with sample sizes of at least $60$ subjects, and for the virtual twins and generalized random forests approaches we restrict to tree depths that involve at most $3$ 
baseline factors}
\begin{enumerate}[{GRF:}]
\item{GRF targets RMST and we denote GRF as RMST based on the truncation point $\tau=\min(\tau_{0},\tau_{1})$ where $\tau_{0}$ and $\tau_1$ are the largest non-censored (event) outcomes for the control and treatment groups (respectively).
An RMST benefit of $6$ months for control is required for selection of an $H$.}
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}[{GRF.70:}]
\item{The GRF procedure employs a double-robust approach for estimating RMST that involves estimation of the censoring distribution.  As such, the choice of the truncation point can be influential.  To reduce the potential instability we consider GRF.70 which uses $\tau_{70}:= 0.7\min(\tau_{0},\tau_{1})$.}
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}[{VT(24):}]
\item{We consider the virtual twins approach targeting survival rates at $t=24$ months. A treatment effect of $\delta=0.25$, in favor of control, is required for selection of an $H$.}
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}[{$VT(24)_{ideal}$:}]
\item{$VT(24)_{ideal}$ denotes virtual twins targeting survival rates at $t=24$ months based on non-censored outcomes.  That is, we remove the challenge of censoring and base analyses on the (ideal) latent outcomes.}
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}[{VT(36):}]
\item{Same as $\hbox{VT(24)}$ but with $t=36$.}
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}[{$VT(36)_{ideal}$:}]
\item{Same as $VT(24)_{ideal}$ but with $t=36$.}
\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}


Table 1 summarizes the proportion of times, across $10,000$ simulations, any subgroup H is identified under the null $H=\emptyset$ and when the hazard ratio for $H$ takes values of $1, 1.5, 2, 2.5,$ and $3.0$.

